WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.148
Logs influence investigations, alerts, audits, reports, and sometimes
automated decisions.

2
00:00:05.148 --> 00:00:06.864
That means logs need integrity.

3
00:00:06.864 --> 00:00:12.584
They should reflect what the application observed without allowing
untrusted data to reshape the record.

4
00:00:12.584 --> 00:00:21.100
If user-controlled text is written directly into free-form log lines, it
may confuse interpretation, break parsing, imitate another event, or hide
context.

5
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.451
The concern is not a payload demonstration.

6
00:00:23.451 --> 00:00:27.010
The concern is that logs are records, and records can be distorted.

7
00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:34.192
Structured logging is a strong defensive pattern because untrusted values
become fields rather than pieces of the log format itself.

8
00:00:34.192 --> 00:00:42.771
Newlines, delimiters, quoting, encoding, and formatting should be handled
by the logging system or serializer instead of handcrafted string
concatenation.

9
00:00:42.771 --> 00:00:49.762
Safe logs make it clear which fields came from users, which came from the
application, and which came from trusted infrastructure.

10
00:00:49.762 --> 00:00:52.177
Timestamp and source integrity also matter.

11
00:00:52.177 --> 00:01:00.757
Investigators need to know when an event occurred, which service produced
it, which environment it came from, and whether clocks are reasonably
synchronized.

12
00:01:00.757 --> 00:01:05.523
In distributed Python systems, a request may pass through several services
and workers.

13
00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:11.497
Consistent timestamps, source fields, and correlation IDs help reconstruct
what happened without guessing.

14
00:01:11.497 --> 00:01:15.120
They also make unusual gaps or impossible sequences easier to spot.

15
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.696
Protecting logs from unauthorized modification or deletion is part of safe
logging.

16
00:01:19.696 --> 00:01:31.072
Append-only storage, tamper-evident designs, restricted write permissions,
controlled administrative access, backups, and separation between
producers and log administrators can all help at a high level.

17
00:01:31.072 --> 00:01:41.240
Teams should not blindly trust every logged field, especially fields
derived from untrusted input, but the logging pipeline itself should be
trustworthy enough to support investigation.
